1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,331
Austria called her its Mona Lisa.

2
00:00:03,201 --> 00:00:07,181
For more than sixty years, she
hung in a palace museum in Vienna,

3
00:00:07,201 --> 00:00:09,535
one of the most famous
faces in the country.

4
00:00:10,275 --> 00:00:13,448
What the museum didn't
say was how she got there.

5
00:00:14,188 --> 00:00:20,169
In 1941, a Nazi lawyer delivered her,
with a letter signed Heil Hitler.

6
00:00:21,169 --> 00:00:26,822
And in 1998, an 82-year-old woman
in Los Angeles asked for her back.

7
00:00:27,822 --> 00:00:33,882
This is the story of Adele Bloch-Bauer,
the woman Gustav Klimt painted in gold,

8
00:00:33,902 --> 00:00:36,352
and of the fight to
give her back her name.

9
00:00:38,612 --> 00:00:40,562
Vienna, 1899.

10
00:00:41,032 --> 00:00:46,672
Adele Bauer is eighteen when she marries
Ferdinand Bloch, a sugar industrialist,

11
00:00:46,692 --> 00:00:47,942
much older than her.

12
00:00:48,478 --> 00:00:52,218
They come from two of the most
prominent Jewish families in the city.

13
00:00:52,238 --> 00:00:54,538
Her sister has already
married his brother.

14
00:00:54,558 --> 00:00:59,130
Later, to keep both names alive,
they join them: Bloch-Bauer.

15
00:00:59,870 --> 00:01:01,690
Adele runs a salon.

16
00:01:01,710 --> 00:01:05,850
Writers, politicians and artists
come to her house to talk.

17
00:01:05,870 --> 00:01:10,010
One of the artists she knows is the
most talked-about painter in Vienna.

18
00:01:10,030 --> 00:01:11,280
Gustav Klimt.

19
00:01:11,977 --> 00:01:16,299
In the summer of 1903, Ferdinand
has an idea for a present.

20
00:01:16,319 --> 00:01:19,317
Adele's parents have a wedding
anniversary in October.

21
00:01:19,337 --> 00:01:22,311
He'll give them a portrait
of their daughter, by Klimt.

22
00:01:23,051 --> 00:01:24,311
October came.

23
00:01:24,331 --> 00:01:26,244
The portrait didn't.

24
00:01:26,264 --> 00:01:28,391
Klimt was famously slow.

25
00:01:28,411 --> 00:01:30,655
This one would take him four years.

26
00:01:32,555 --> 00:01:34,455
He started with drawings.

27
00:01:34,475 --> 00:01:39,175
Adele seated, Adele
standing, Adele turning away.

28
00:01:39,195 --> 00:01:41,798
More than a hundred of
these studies survive.

29
00:01:42,538 --> 00:01:48,118
Then, in December 1903, Klimt
travelled to Ravenna, in Italy,

30
00:01:48,138 --> 00:01:50,875
to see the mosaics in
the church of San Vitale.

31
00:01:51,515 --> 00:01:56,135
Saints and an empress, Theodora,
glittering on walls of gold,

32
00:01:56,155 --> 00:01:58,615
more than thirteen centuries old.

33
00:01:58,635 --> 00:02:01,943
He called them mosaics of
unprecedented splendour.

34
00:02:02,943 --> 00:02:08,763
Back in Vienna, he painted Adele's face,
shoulders and hands like a portrait.

35
00:02:08,783 --> 00:02:12,283
Everything else, he built
out of gold and silver leaf,

36
00:02:12,303 --> 00:02:14,733
with patterns raised from the surface.

37
00:02:15,343 --> 00:02:17,883
Her dress is covered in eyes and spirals.

38
00:02:17,903 --> 00:02:20,043
Her chair dissolves into the wall.

39
00:02:20,063 --> 00:02:22,443
The museum that owns her today says

40
00:02:22,463 --> 00:02:24,960
you can't even tell if
she's standing or sitting.

41
00:02:25,700 --> 00:02:27,280
And look at her hands.

42
00:02:27,300 --> 00:02:30,210
They're twisted together,
almost awkwardly.

43
00:02:30,385 --> 00:02:33,760
Adele had a damaged finger
from a childhood accident.

44
00:02:33,780 --> 00:02:35,668
Some writers think she's hiding it.

45
00:02:36,408 --> 00:02:38,838
The portrait was finished in 1907.

46
00:02:39,128 --> 00:02:40,868
Critics were divided.

47
00:02:40,888 --> 00:02:44,788
One Vienna paper called
it Mehr Blech als Bloch.

48
00:02:44,808 --> 00:02:46,764
More brass than Bloch.

49
00:02:48,664 --> 00:02:51,364
Ferdinand and Adele were clearly pleased.

50
00:02:51,384 --> 00:02:56,404
They gave Klimt an unprecedented second
commission: another portrait of Adele,

51
00:02:56,424 --> 00:02:58,116
finished in 1912.

52
00:02:58,856 --> 00:03:03,876
Six years later, in February
1918, Klimt died of a stroke.

53
00:03:03,896 --> 00:03:05,446
He was 55.

54
00:03:06,317 --> 00:03:09,657
In 1923, Adele wrote her will.

55
00:03:09,677 --> 00:03:11,497
In it, there's one sentence

56
00:03:11,517 --> 00:03:14,738
that would matter more than
anything else she ever wrote.

57
00:03:15,218 --> 00:03:18,958
I ask my husband after his
death to leave my two portraits

58
00:03:18,978 --> 00:03:24,053
and the four landscapes by Gustav Klimt
to the Austrian State Gallery in Vienna.

59
00:03:25,053 --> 00:03:30,523
Two years later, in January
1925, Adele died of meningitis.

60
00:03:30,733 --> 00:03:32,135
She was 43.

61
00:03:33,005 --> 00:03:37,305
Ferdinand kept her room as a memorial,
with her Klimts on the walls.

62
00:03:37,325 --> 00:03:40,345
He told the court he
meant to honour her wish.

63
00:03:40,365 --> 00:03:42,088
He never got the chance.

64
00:03:43,988 --> 00:03:48,688
In December 1937, Adele's
niece Maria, twenty-one,

65
00:03:48,708 --> 00:03:51,088
married a young man called Fritz Altmann.

66
00:03:51,108 --> 00:03:55,006
Ferdinand gave her a wedding
present: Adele's diamond necklace.

67
00:03:55,746 --> 00:03:59,006
Three months later, German
troops crossed the border.

68
00:03:59,026 --> 00:04:01,726
Austria became part of Hitler's Reich.

69
00:04:01,746 --> 00:04:06,686
The music you're hearing was recorded
in Vienna that January, weeks before.

70
00:04:06,706 --> 00:04:10,144
Its conductor, Bruno Walter,
would soon have to flee.

71
00:04:11,014 --> 00:04:13,314
The Gestapo took the necklace.

72
00:04:13,334 --> 00:04:16,594
They arrested Maria's husband
and sent him to Dachau,

73
00:04:16,614 --> 00:04:20,073
to force his brother to hand over
the family's textile business.

74
00:04:20,553 --> 00:04:23,813
When he was released, Maria and Fritz ran.

75
00:04:23,833 --> 00:04:28,743
A farmer led them across a stream and
under barbed wire, into the Netherlands.

76
00:04:28,953 --> 00:04:31,733
From there, England, then America.

77
00:04:31,753 --> 00:04:35,868
Maria ended up in Los Angeles,
selling clothes from her home.

78
00:04:36,689 --> 00:04:41,389
Ferdinand fled too, first to
Czechoslovakia, then to Switzerland.

79
00:04:41,409 --> 00:04:46,269
The Nazis accused him of tax evasion and
seized everything he had left behind:

80
00:04:46,289 --> 00:04:49,729
his sugar factory, his homes, his art.

81
00:04:50,469 --> 00:04:55,969
A Nazi lawyer, Erich Führer, was
put in charge of selling it off.

82
00:04:55,989 --> 00:05:02,209
In 1941, he traded the golden portrait to
Austria's national gallery, the Belvedere,

83
00:05:02,229 --> 00:05:04,529
for a Klimt landscape the museum owned,

84
00:05:04,549 --> 00:05:07,332
which went to a film director
called Gustav Ucicky.

85
00:05:07,862 --> 00:05:10,592
Ucicky made films for the Nazis.

86
00:05:10,612 --> 00:05:13,742
And by most accounts,
he was Klimt's own son.

87
00:05:14,742 --> 00:05:18,242
The portrait arrived with a
note: it was being handed over

88
00:05:18,262 --> 00:05:21,202
according to the wishes
of Adele Bloch-Bauer.

89
00:05:21,222 --> 00:05:23,434
Signed, Heil Hitler.

90
00:05:24,434 --> 00:05:28,174
A Jewish woman's name couldn't
hang in a museum of the Reich.

91
00:05:28,194 --> 00:05:30,254
So the gallery gave her a new one.

92
00:05:30,274 --> 00:05:31,927
The Lady in Gold.

93
00:05:33,187 --> 00:05:35,327
Ferdinand never saw her again.

94
00:05:35,347 --> 00:05:40,127
He died in Switzerland in
November 1945, almost penniless.

95
00:05:40,147 --> 00:05:45,407
In his last will, he left everything
he had to his nephew and two nieces.

96
00:05:45,427 --> 00:05:46,826
One of them was Maria.

97
00:05:48,726 --> 00:05:54,306
After the war, Austria passed a law
cancelling the sales forced on Jewish owners.

98
00:05:54,326 --> 00:05:57,506
But when the family asked for
the Klimts, they were told

99
00:05:57,526 --> 00:06:00,014
Adele's will had already given them away.

100
00:06:00,624 --> 00:06:04,684
And Jewish families who wanted to take
their other property out of Austria

101
00:06:04,704 --> 00:06:08,124
needed export permits,
which often came at a price:

102
00:06:08,144 --> 00:06:11,454
art, left behind for Austria's museums.

103
00:06:11,664 --> 00:06:15,673
In 1948, the family's lawyer
signed the Klimts over.

104
00:06:16,413 --> 00:06:19,353
The Lady in Gold became
the star of the Belvedere.

105
00:06:19,373 --> 00:06:21,353
Austria's Mona Lisa.

106
00:06:21,373 --> 00:06:24,793
And at some point, the gallery's
own records were rewritten,

107
00:06:24,813 --> 00:06:28,020
to suggest the portraits had
arrived before the Nazis did.

108
00:06:29,020 --> 00:06:35,640
Then, in 1998, Austria passed a new law
on looted art, and opened its archives.

109
00:06:35,660 --> 00:06:39,281
A journalist, Hubertus
Czernin, started reading.

110
00:06:39,761 --> 00:06:41,981
He found the Nazi lawyer's letter.

111
00:06:42,001 --> 00:06:45,941
And papers showing that Ferdinand
had never given the paintings away.

112
00:06:46,681 --> 00:06:49,461
They had never even been Adele's to give.

113
00:06:49,481 --> 00:06:51,221
Ferdinand had paid for them.

114
00:06:51,241 --> 00:06:52,821
They were his.

115
00:06:52,841 --> 00:06:56,101
Her famous sentence
was a wish, not a gift.

116
00:06:56,121 --> 00:06:59,471
And Ferdinand's own will left
everything to his family.

117
00:07:01,421 --> 00:07:03,721
Maria Altmann was now 82.

118
00:07:03,771 --> 00:07:07,655
She called a young lawyer whose
grandmother had been her best friend:

119
00:07:07,675 --> 00:07:11,751
Randol Schoenberg, grandson of
the composer Arnold Schoenberg,

120
00:07:11,771 --> 00:07:14,572
another Viennese who had fled the Nazis.

121
00:07:15,182 --> 00:07:19,822
In 1999, Austria's restitution
committee turned her down.

122
00:07:19,842 --> 00:07:21,978
Adele's will, it said.

123
00:07:22,588 --> 00:07:24,408
She could sue in Austria.

124
00:07:24,428 --> 00:07:27,528
But Austrian court fees were
a share of what was at stake.

125
00:07:27,548 --> 00:07:32,328
For these paintings, about one and
a half million dollars, up front.

126
00:07:32,348 --> 00:07:34,882
So she sued Austria in Los Angeles.

127
00:07:35,622 --> 00:07:39,282
Austria said a foreign country
can't be sued in an American court.

128
00:07:39,302 --> 00:07:43,122
The fight went all the way to
the United States Supreme Court.

129
00:07:43,142 --> 00:07:47,122
In June 2004, it ruled six to three.

130
00:07:47,142 --> 00:07:48,392
She could sue.

131
00:07:49,132 --> 00:07:53,832
Then both sides agreed to let
three Austrian arbitrators decide.

132
00:07:53,852 --> 00:07:57,512
In January 2006, they ruled.

133
00:07:57,532 --> 00:08:02,494
Five paintings had to go back to the
family, including both portraits of Adele.

134
00:08:03,364 --> 00:08:05,104
Austria could have bought them.

135
00:08:05,124 --> 00:08:08,944
Maria had even said she'd
like the portraits to stay.

136
00:08:08,964 --> 00:08:13,609
The government said it couldn't afford
the price: around 300 million dollars.

137
00:08:14,479 --> 00:08:17,699
Before she left, posters
went up across Vienna.

138
00:08:17,719 --> 00:08:19,150
Ciao Adele.

139
00:08:21,050 --> 00:08:27,190
In March 2006, the paintings left Austria
for Los Angeles, where they went on show.

140
00:08:27,210 --> 00:08:31,190
That June, Ronald Lauder,
heir to a cosmetics fortune,

141
00:08:31,210 --> 00:08:35,430
bought the golden portrait for his
museum in New York, the Neue Galerie,

142
00:08:35,450 --> 00:08:40,725
for 135 million dollars, then the
highest price ever paid for a painting.

143
00:08:41,205 --> 00:08:43,525
He called her our Mona Lisa.

144
00:08:44,395 --> 00:08:48,775
Maria Altmann died in 2011,
at home in Los Angeles.

145
00:08:48,795 --> 00:08:50,265
She was 94.

146
00:08:50,395 --> 00:08:55,095
Four years later, Hollywood told her
story, with Helen Mirren as Maria.

147
00:08:55,115 --> 00:08:56,365
Woman in Gold.

148
00:08:57,546 --> 00:09:00,916
Adele asked for her portraits
to end up in a museum.

149
00:09:00,936 --> 00:09:03,556
In the end, the golden one did.

150
00:09:03,576 --> 00:09:08,215
A museum anyone can walk into,
where the label says who she was.

151
00:09:08,235 --> 00:09:09,980
Adele Bloch-Bauer.

152
00:09:11,500 --> 00:09:14,280
It hangs in the Neue Galerie, in New York.
